  "findings": [
    {
      "evidence": "template/scripts/requirement_terminal.py:395-396 and 78. `publication_queue.py:182-194` handles the same kind of marker differently. It restricts the association to MEMBER or COLLABORATOR and then checks `collaborators/{login}/permission` against `_WRITER_PERMISSIONS`, failing closed. The new gate skips that check. design.md says the trusted set is 'the backlog's writers', but the code does not verify that.",
      "id": "acceptance-trust-weaker-than-queue-writer-check",
      "severity": "advisory",
      "summary": "The acceptance decision marker is trusted by `author_association` alone (OWNER, MEMBER or COLLABORATOR). Those values do not prove write access. A read-only org member or collaborator could post a marker that confirms or waives an owner gate. The marker's `actor` field is also self-asserted in the comment body."
    },
    {
      "evidence": "template/scripts/lifecycle_overview.py, `_observe_requirement`: `intake.fetch_issue(...)` and `terminal.open_acceptance_items(...)`. template/scripts/requirement_terminal.py: `reason.strip()[:300]` in `accept`.",
      "id": "overview-uncached-per-requirement-gh-reads",
      "severity": "advisory",
      "summary": "`_observe_requirement` now makes two extra uncached GitHub calls for every non-closed Requirement on each overview run. These are `fetch_issue` and, when the section is declared, a paginated `issue_comments`. The module already has a `_cached_read` helper for observations, which this code does not use. `accept` also silently truncates the owner's reason to 300 characters."
    }
